AFS-002 — Identity & Namespace Model

Document ID: AFS-002
Title: Identity & Namespace Model
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Identity and Namespace Model (CINM) for the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Canonical Identity and Namespace Model establishes the principles, structures, governance, and resolution mechanisms for uniquely identifying every Atlas artifact while enabling scalable organization, federation, interoperability, and long-term stability.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Canonical identities
Namespace architecture
Qualified names
Identity lifecycle
Identity resolution
Namespace governance
Identity interoperability
Federation support

This specification applies to every Atlas artifact.

3. Canonical Definition

A Canonical Identity is a globally unique, immutable, and persistent identifier assigned to an Atlas artifact. A Namespace is a governed logical domain that provides contextual uniqueness, ownership, and organizational structure for canonical identities.

Identity identifies.

Namespaces organize.

Neither SHALL define behavior.

4. Objectives

The Canonical Identity and Namespace Model SHALL:

Guarantee global uniqueness.
Preserve long-term stability.
Enable deterministic identity resolution.
Support federation.
Prevent identifier collisions.
Support implementation independence.
Enable interoperability.
Preserve traceability.
5. Identity Principles

Every Atlas identity SHALL satisfy the following principles.

5.1 Global Uniqueness

Every Canonical Identity SHALL be globally unique.

No two Atlas artifacts SHALL share the same Canonical Identity.

5.2 Immutability

Once assigned, a Canonical Identity SHALL NEVER change.

Artifact attributes MAY evolve.

Identity SHALL remain constant.

5.3 Persistence

Canonical Identities SHALL remain valid throughout the lifetime of the artifact, including archival.

5.4 Independence

Canonical Identities SHALL NOT encode:

Implementation details
Database keys
Storage locations
Organization-specific formats
5.5 Resolution

Every Canonical Identity SHALL be resolvable through governed identity mechanisms.

5.6 Federation

Identity SHALL remain globally unique across all Atlas federations.

6. Identity Architecture
Atlas Federation
        │
        ▼
Namespace
        │
        ▼
Canonical Identity
        │
        ▼
Atlas Artifact

Identity SHALL be independent of artifact type.

7. Canonical Identity Structure

Every Canonical Identity SHALL define:

Component	Description
Identity	Immutable unique identifier
Namespace	Governing namespace
Artifact Type	Classification reference
Version Reference	Applicable version (where relevant)
Lifecycle Status	Current identity status
Authority	Issuing authority

The identity itself SHALL remain immutable.

Associated metadata MAY evolve.

8. Namespace Model

Atlas namespaces SHALL provide logical ownership.

Example hierarchy:

atlas
├── foundation
├── architecture
├── data
├── policy
├── workflow
├── intelligence
├── organization
├── project
└── extension

Organizations MAY create governed child namespaces.

9. Qualified Names

Atlas SHALL support qualified names.

Example:

atlas.workflow.foundation.review.approval

Qualified names SHALL:

Be human-readable.
Be hierarchical.
Support deterministic resolution.
Remain namespace-qualified.

Qualified names SHALL NOT replace Canonical Identities.

10. Identity Lifecycle

Every Canonical Identity SHALL support:

Reserved
    │
Assigned
    │
Active
    │
Deprecated
    │
Archived

Identity retirement SHALL NOT permit reuse.

11. Identity Resolution

Atlas SHALL support deterministic identity resolution.

Resolution SHALL support:

Canonical Identity
Qualified Name
Namespace
Version
Alias (where governed)

Resolution SHALL return exactly one authoritative artifact or an explicit resolution failure.

12. Identity Governance

Every namespace SHALL define:

Namespace authority
Registration policy
Delegation rules
Conflict resolution
Lifecycle policy
Retention requirements

Namespace governance SHALL remain independently auditable.

13. Identity Quality

Every Canonical Identity SHALL satisfy:

Uniqueness
Stability
Persistence
Traceability
Determinism
Interoperability
Scalability
Auditability

Quality SHALL be continuously preserved.

14. Conformance

An implementation conforms if it:

Generates globally unique identities.
Preserves identity immutability.
Implements governed namespaces.
Supports deterministic resolution.
Prevents identity reuse.
Maintains federation compatibility.
15. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Distributed identity registries.
Cryptographically verifiable identities.
Decentralized namespace authorities.
Semantic identity resolution.
Machine-verifiable identity proofs.
Cross-standard identity mappings.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical identity semantics.

